  <Section tone="paper" padding="default" id="readiness" class="scroll-mt-24 border-t border-line">
    <Container width="content">
      <div class="case-layout">
        <div class="case-layout__head">
          <p class="mono-label mb-3">Step 02</p>
          <Heading level={2} visual="display-md" tone="ink">The readiness gate before scheduling</Heading>
        </div>
        <div class="case-layout__body">
          <div class="mt-8 measure space-y-5 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted text-lg">
            <p>
              I asked all three interviewees what they'd fix first, and all three named
              the same thing: a shared readiness workflow that both facilities confirm
              <em>before</em> a courier is scheduled. That convergence reorganized the
              whole product.
            </p>
            <p>
              The worst operational pattern, Daryl told me, is "scheduling first and
              hoping the remaining requirements are completed before pickup." It happens
              because someone is protecting a patient's transfer date; sometimes it works,
              and sometimes it becomes a last-minute cancellation, wasted courier time, and
              more anxiety. So I designed readiness as a
              <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">formal state, not a general
              impression</strong>, and built the system to keep a requested date, a
              provisional plan, and a confirmed shipment as three distinct things,
              where a paper process let them all look the same.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-10 rounded-lg border border-line p-6 md:p-8">
            <p class="mono-label mb-6">Every party clears its checkpoint before the case can be scheduled</p>
            <ul>
              {readinessGate.map((row, i) => (
                <li class="flex items-center gap-4 border-b border-line py-3.5 last:border-b-0">
                  <Icon name="ph:seal-check" size={18} class="shrink-0 text-accent" />
                  <span class="w-36 shrink-0 font-medium text-ink">{row.party}</span>
                  <span class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-snug">{row.check}</span>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
            <p class="mt-5 text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">
              Only when every checkpoint is acknowledged does the case move from
              <span class="font-mono text-xs text-ink">awaiting_readiness</span> to
              <span class="font-mono text-xs text-ink">ready</span>, and only then can it
              be scheduled. It was the first requirement I handed engineering, and the one
              I most wanted the data model to enforce for me, because the failure it
              prevents, a courier rolling before a clinic was really ready, is the one that
              ends in a call to a patient two days before their transfer date.
            </p>
          </div>

          <figure class="my-12 border-l-2 border-accent pl-6">
            <blockquote class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl tracking-tight text-ink leading-snug">
              It would not be enough for our team to mark a clinic as ready. The
              responsible person at each facility would confirm that the documents, staff,
              equipment, and timing are in place.
            </blockquote>
            <figcaption class="mt-4 text-sm text-ink-muted">Sasha Jenkins · Office Manager, IVFCRYO</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section tone="paper" padding="default" id="flows" class="scroll-mt-24 border-t border-line">
    <Container width="content">
      <div class="case-layout">
        <div class="case-layout__head">
          <p class="mono-label mb-3">Step 03</p>
          <Heading level={2} visual="display-md" tone="ink">Designing both sides, and the public view</Heading>
        </div>
        <div class="case-layout__body">
          <div class="mt-8 measure space-y-5 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted text-lg">
            <p>
              One shipment has very different users. The customer, often a clinic moving
              fast or a patient touching an emotionally loaded process for the first time,
              needs a short, reassuring path from request to tracking. The IVFCRYO admin
              lives in the tool all day and needs range: every shipment, every client, and
              enough control to resolve the exceptions.
            </p>
            <p>
              Two apps was the obvious shape: one for customers, one for the admin team,
              each simpler to build in isolation. I rejected it for the same reason the
              product exists at all: two apps means two records of one shipment, and the
              moment they drift apart, the interface reintroduces the exact ambiguity
              ("sent" isn't "received and approved") that breaks trust at every handoff in
              the paper process. So I mapped two sitemaps off one shared home instead, the
              shipping list, and let the information architecture fork from there, one
              shipment record underneath both. A readiness view now sits on the customer
              side, because Sasha's hardest daily work was chasing "what are we still
              waiting for?" across email, fax, and clinic portals. Same spine, different
              reach.
            </p>
