  <Section tone="paper" padding="default" id="recovery" class="scroll-mt-24 border-t border-line">
    <Container width="content">
      <div class="case-layout">
        <div class="case-layout__head">
          <p class="mono-label mb-3">Step 04</p>
          <Heading level={2} visual="display-md" tone="ink">Making the interface a recovery system</Heading>
        </div>
        <div class="case-layout__body">
          <div class="mt-8 measure space-y-5 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted text-lg">
            <p>
              The old process created too many chances for human error, and in this
              domain a small one travels a long way: a mistyped address or a name that
              does not match a clinic's records is not a form problem, it is a risk to
              someone's only embryo. So I treated the interface as a recovery system, not
              just a form system: help people avoid errors, understand them when they
              happen, and move forward without calling support for every correction.
              Specimen and package details were captured as structured records, addresses
              were geocoded and validated, and critical actions got a confirmation point.
              The rule was simple:
              <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">make the correct path easier than the incorrect one.</strong>
            </p>
            <p>
              The research gave that rule a second job. IVFCRYO's service guarantee,
              <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">CRYO Shield</strong>, turns
              documentation into part of the safety system rather than paperwork added
              after the fact. A qualifying claim needs an error-free order, documented
              handoffs, no preexisting damage, and an arrival temperature warmer than
              &minus;130&deg;C at specimen-storage level, filed within 30 days. As Daryl
              said, "the claims process begins long before a claim exists." So the order
              form captures a declared value and protection tier, and receiving captures a
              calibrated temperature and condition photos immediately, because if the
              record is incomplete, the event can't be reconstructed later.
            </p>
            <p>
              Accessibility was part of the same strategy, not a later pass, and not a
              single round of feedback either. A legally blind customer collaborated from
              the beginning, testing flows with a screen reader while the product was
              still being shaped, and stayed involved after launch: the multi-carrier
              labeling gap in
              <a href="#edges" class="hero-link">the edges below</a>
              only surfaced because that same kind of testing kept running against real
              releases. Status, errors, and alerts carried text, icon, and shape, never
              color alone. When something appeared or disappeared on screen, the page
              kept track of where a keyboard user's attention should land next, and
              important updates were read aloud automatically instead of only
              appearing silently on screen.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-10 rounded-lg border border-line p-6 md:p-8">
            <p class="mono-label mb-5">Built-in, not bolted-on</p>
            <ul class="grid gap-x-8 gap-y-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
