  <Section tone="paper" padding="default" id="architecture" class="scroll-mt-24 border-t border-line">
    <Container width="content">
      <div class="case-layout">
        <div class="case-layout__head">
          <p class="mono-label mb-3">Step 05</p>
          <Heading level={2} visual="display-md" tone="ink">Partnering on the system underneath</Heading>
        </div>
        <div class="case-layout__body">
          <div class="mt-8 measure space-y-5 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted text-lg">
            <p>
              A logistics product's front end can only promise what the backend can
              deliver, so I didn't design the interface and throw it over a wall. I owned
              the front end, and I worked alongside the engineering team on the domain
              model that had to carry it.
            </p>
            <p>
              My contribution to the backend was design-driven. I translated the workflow
              research into the lifecycle states and the readiness gate the data had to
              enforce, drew where each carrier and service boundary should sit so vendor
              quirks never leaked into the interface, and set the monitoring and
              alerting requirements the cold chain depended on. The engineers owned
              the backend implementation. I owned the front-end component system
              built against it: reusable building blocks that already work correctly
              before all of a page's scripts finish loading, so a new screen
              assembles from pieces that are already accessible instead of being
              built from scratch. I also owned the specification that system had to
              answer to.
            </p>
            <p>
              That is the seam a
              <strong class="text-ink font-semibold">product design engineer</strong> is
              meant to hold: fluent enough in the backend to shape the model that keeps the
              interface's promises, and honest enough to know the difference between
              shaping it and owning it.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-10 rounded-lg border border-line p-6 md:p-8">
            <p class="mono-label mb-6">The integration boundaries we drew together</p>
            <ul class="grid gap-x-8 gap-y-5 sm:grid-cols-2">
              {integrations.map((svc) => (
                <li class="flex gap-3.5">
                  <Icon name={svc.icon} size={22} class="mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-ink-muted" />
